I just wanted to say that I'm rather in agreement here.

Previous years, I have attempted to compete for the "most ascensions"
award.  That was fun, but I can't do so this year -- while marvin has
skill on his side, I have to rely on sheer persistence ... and I don't
have time to devote four or five hours a day to nethack this november.

(Hopefully that doesn't come off as a complaint.  If I *had* the time,
you can bet I'd be playing -- I might not have won the tournaments I
played in, but I sure had a lot of fun.)

Such as it is, there's nothing to really draw me to the tournament this
year.  The vast majority of the awards involve large amounts of playing
time, and I enjoy the tournament because of the competition.  If I'm not
competing, then there's not much of a reason to join the tournament.

(I will probably register and play a few games when I get a chance;
finding interesting sets of bones is always a plus when playing
nethack.)

With respect to your ideas for prizes, there are some good ideas there.
They would not have to necessarily be "trophy spots"; having your name
up in lights would be enough of an incentive.  For me, I just think it
would be fun to have something to aim for, that doesn't depend on
playing hundreds of games or for dozens of hours. (Conducts have been
mentioned as a possibility for next year, right? -- that sounds like
a great start to me.)

On a slightly different note, I think I mentioned once before that it
would be very nice to have the full record files available after the
tournament.  I still think so :)  Given that the "top 500" doesn't even
show all of the ascensions during a year, having the full records
files would be an item of interest.

